## Final Verdict: Which Color Should You Buy?

**Buyer 1 — White gold or platinum setting, natural stone:** F is your target, G is your floor. At 1ct, [F-VS1 at $2,521](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29365844?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the benchmark. At 2ct, [F-VS1 at $21,213](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27103709?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and [G-VS2 at $21,147](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27103714?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) are essentially the same price — take F. Do not buy H in white metal and assume you will not notice. You will notice.

**Buyer 2 — Yellow gold setting, natural stone:** H is acceptable, G is comfortable. The yellow metal gives you one full grade of forgiveness. If your budget is tight, [E-VS1 at $2,464](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28919719?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) in yellow gold is almost overkill for color — the setting masks far more than E's tiny warmth. You could spend that color premium on a size upgrade instead.

**Buyer 3 — Lab-grown asscher, any setting:** Buy D color. The [1.52ct D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the clearest value in this category — D color in a stone nearly 60% larger than a natural 1ct, for near the same price. The Color Corridor Effect rewards D color in the step facets every single day. There is no color compromise that makes sense when D is this accessible.

**Buyer 4 — Budget buyer, any setting:** If the budget forces a G choice in white gold, go G-VS1 or G-VS2 with photo review. At 1ct, G in an asscher is near-colorless to most people in most lighting. The Color Corridor Effect makes it visible to trained eyes, but most daily wear situations will not reveal it. Do not drop to H in white gold to save money — the warmth in an H asscher under platinum is visible in any natural light and you will see it every morning.

> **Farzana says:** Step cuts are honest. They show you everything — the depth, the architecture, the inclusions, and yes, the color. The buyers who save on color and buy H in platinum regret it. The buyers who buy F in yellow gold paid for something the setting hides. Know your metal before you set your color grade. That one rule saves more regret than any other piece of advice I give.

